--- a/docs/docs/image/resize.mdx
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,66 +0,0 @@
----
-title: Resize
----
-
-import Lang from '@site/src/components/Lang';
-import Compare from '@site/src/components/Compare';
-import Bench from '@site/src/components/Bench';
-import ApiLink from '@site/src/components/ApiLink';
-
-`resize(width, height)` returns a new image of the requested size. The image you call it on is not changed.
-
-<Lang name="resize" />
-
-The code shrinks `astronaut.png` to 64×64 with the default filter, then enlarges that small picture to
-256×256 once per filter. The small picture is the poorest possible input on purpose, so the differences
-between filters are easy to see.
-
-## What it produces
-
-These pictures are the output of the snippet above, not separate artwork.
-
-<Compare
-  size={192}
-  items={[
-    { src: 'img/generated/resize/tiny.png', label: '64×64, default filter (shown at 3×)', pixelated: true },
-    { src: 'img/generated/resize/nearest.png', label: 'nearest' },
-    { src: 'img/generated/resize/linear.png', label: 'linear' },
-    { src: 'img/generated/resize/cubic.png', label: 'cubic' },
-    { src: 'img/generated/resize/bspline.png', label: 'bspline' },
-    { src: 'img/generated/resize/mitchell.png', label: 'mitchell' },
-  ]}
-/>
-
-## Choosing the filter
-
-The filter is picked by name or by enum. With no filter, libstb uses Catmull-Rom when it enlarges and
-Mitchell when it shrinks. Names are case-insensitive, and `-` or a space counts as `_`.
-
-| Filter | Names |
-| --- | --- |
-| default | `auto`, `default` |
-| nearest | `nearest`, `point` |
-| linear | `linear`, `bilinear`, `triangle` |
-| cubic (Catmull-Rom) | `cubic`, `bicubic`, `catmull_rom` |
-| B-spline | `bspline`, `cubic_bspline` |
-| Mitchell | `mitchell` |
-| box | `box`, `area` |
-
-There is no Lanczos filter. In Python an unknown name raises `ValueError` and lists the valid names.
-
-By default libstb blends pixels in linear light. `Resizer(filter, srgb=False)` turns that off, which is the
-like-for-like setting when comparing with libraries that do not do it.
-
-## How fast is it
-
-The reference in these two charts is OpenCV, the library that was fastest on the most pictures. On average
-libstb, with either setting, takes longer than OpenCV here, and less time than Pillow and scikit-image.
-The note under each chart explains why OpenCV's linear and cubic modes are not a like-for-like comparison
-when shrinking.
-
-<Bench section="resizing" ops={['resize_down_cubic', 'resize_up_cubic']} />
-
-## Reference
-
-- <ApiLink name="image.resize" />
-- <ApiLink name="resizer" />
