+---
+id: resize
+title: Resize
+---
+
+import Lang from '@site/src/components/Lang';
+import Only from '@site/src/components/Only';
+import Differ from '@site/src/components/Differ';
+import Compare from '@site/src/components/Compare';
+import Bench from '@site/src/components/Bench';
+
+`resize(width, height)` returns a new image of the requested size. The image you call it on is not changed.
+
+<Lang name="resize" />
+
+The code shrinks `astronaut.png` to 64×64 with the default filter, then enlarges that small picture to 256×256 once per filter. The small picture is the poorest possible input on purpose, so the differences between filters are easy to see.
+
+## What it produces
+
+These pictures are the output of the example above, not separate artwork.
+
+<Compare
+  size={192}
+  items={[
+    { src: 'img/resize/tiny.png', label: '64×64, default filter (shown at 3×)', pixelated: true },
+    { src: 'img/resize/nearest.png', label: 'nearest' },
+    { src: 'img/resize/linear.png', label: 'linear' },
+    { src: 'img/resize/cubic.png', label: 'cubic' },
+    { src: 'img/resize/bspline.png', label: 'bspline' },
+    { src: 'img/resize/mitchell.png', label: 'mitchell' },
+  ]}
+/>
+
+## Choosing the filter
+
+The filter is picked by name or by enum. With no filter, libstb uses Catmull-Rom when it enlarges and Mitchell when it shrinks. Names are case-insensitive, and `-` or a space counts as `_`.
+
+| Filter | Names |
+| --- | --- |
+| default | `auto`, `default` |
+| nearest | `nearest`, `point` |
+| linear | `linear`, `bilinear`, `triangle` |
+| cubic (Catmull-Rom) | `cubic`, `bicubic`, `catmull_rom` |
+| B-spline | `bspline`, `cubic_bspline` |
+| Mitchell | `mitchell` |
+| box | `box`, `area` |
+
+There is no Lanczos filter. <Only lang="python">An unknown name raises `ValueError` and lists the valid names.</Only>
+
+By default libstb blends pixels in linear light. `Resizer(filter, srgb=False)` turns that off, which is the like-for-like setting when comparing with libraries that do not do it.
+
+## How fast is it
+
+The reference in these two charts is OpenCV, the library that was fastest on the most pictures. On average libstb, with either setting, takes longer than OpenCV here, and less time than Pillow and scikit-image. The note under each chart explains why OpenCV's linear and cubic modes are not a like-for-like comparison when shrinking.
+
+<Bench section="resizing" ops={['resize_down_cubic', 'resize_up_cubic']} />
+
+## API at a glance
+
+<Differ rows={[
+  ['Open a file', '`stb.Image.open(path)`', '`stb::image::open(path)`'],
+  ['Resize', '`img.resize(w, h, filter)`', '`img.resize(w, h, filter)`'],
+  ['Write', '`img.write(path)`', '`img.write(path)`'],
+  ['Pick the filter', 'a name, or a `Resizer.Filter` for `Resizer`; an unknown name raises `ValueError`', 'a name, a `stb::resize_filter`, or a `const stb::resizer*`'],
+  ['Linear-light blending off', '`stb.Resizer(filter, srgb=False)`', '`stb::resizer(filter).srgb(false)`'],
+]} />
